From: "Peter C. McCluskey" <pcm@rahul.net>
Date: Wed, 7 Dec 1994 18:06:19 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-310] upgrading

 M_FISCHER@ACM.ORG (Mark Fischer) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-12-280:
>Simply put: if we were to design a human brain from scratch (in
>broad strokes!), what would we fix/improve? There are without

 I hate the use of the word "design" for something this complex. It
almost has to involve a good deal of evolution and reuse of components.

>question tons of hacks in the existing arrangement. Have we
>evolved (at this point) to _need_ those flaws? Such that we 
>can not fix them without destroying qualities that we would
>like to keep? Is the only way we can "improve" our intelligence
>to raise the "clock speed"?

 My favorite example of such an improvement involves evaluating risks of
improbable events. Psychologists have identified common bugs which produce
clearly irrational behavior. Grossberg argues this is due to a comparison
being made between 2 neurons which represent alternatives. If the time to
generate one alternative is longer than the other, the response level on
the first neuron will have decayed (its "numeric value" reduced), causing
an inaccurate comparison. (See ch. 9 of Grossberg's _Neural Networks and
Natural Intelligence_, or Psych. Review 94.3, p 300).
 It takes too much energy for biological neurons to maintain a firing rate
long enough to solve this, but I am moderately optimistic that fixing
digital neurons by either maintaining the response level longer or
equalizing the response times will have only minor side-affects, but
those side-affects would certainly be widespread.
 I think most other improvements to the human brain will be somewhat
trickier and harder to understand than this.

