From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Wed, 7 Dec 1994 22:15:36 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-317] BASICS: Old-Growth Forestry

>From: Phil Goetz <goetz@cs.Buffalo.EDU>
>Date: Wed, 7 Dec 1994 10:37:13 -0500
>Subject: [#94-12-279] Privatization of National Parks.
>
>...when preservation is by private individuals, each person
>can at any time decide to "develop" whatever resource he's got.  For a
>redwood forest to grow undisturbed for 500 years, you would need every
>individual who ever owned that forest to decide every year for 500 years
>that it was more advantageous to keep it uncut.  Just one swing in the
>market in 500 years that makes cutting profitable is all it takes to
>undercut hundreds of years of conservation.

"When preservation is by States, each State can at any time decide to
'develop' whatever resource it's got.  For a redwood forest to grow
undisturbed for 500 years, you would need ever State who ever owned that
forest to decide every year for 500 years that it was more advantageous
to keep it uncut.  Just one swing in the State's ravenous appetitite for
revenue to fill the bellies of the parasitic ruling class in 500 years
that makes cutting preferable is all it takes to undercut hundreds of
years of conservation."

Why is it that some people come up with all sorts of reasons why markets
must fail, but never consider the remotest possibility that the State might
fail to do what they want it to do?

>Yes, burning is natural.  BUT:
>1.  Burning is only helpful to quick-growth species like spruce.

Wrong.  Some tree species (like Giant Sequoia) need fire in order to
release their seeds.

>Forests do not get "too old".

They sure as hell do!

>...Have you ever seen an old-growth forest?

Yep: Giant Sequoia Nat'l Park, Muir Woods, etc.

>...They have little undergrowth to spread fire,
>mostly just canopy cover.  They have reached a stable population,
>so there is no buildup of combustible materials.

In other words, they become a monoculture, with all the advantages &
disadvantages of any other kind of monoculture.  For instance, big
trees are more vulnerable to high winds than smaller ones, so old-
growth forests are more likely to be devastated by tornados, hurricanes,
etc., than non-monoculture forests.  They also become more vulnerable
to disease, the lack of undergrowth means they harbor less diverse flora
& fauna (because they have less food & shelter)...

>I doubt that you could "manage" an old forest (meaning cutting down parts
>of it periodically), because old-growth trees and new-growth trees are
>incompatible.  The youngsters would be in the shadows of the old giants,
>and do poorly; if some did well, they would increase the likelihood of
>fire and endanger the old trees.

If you don't "manage" old forests, they eventually all die off at once.
It may take a long time, but they all go together when they go.  Then
you don't have any old trees left, whereas if you regularly cut down
parts and let new trees grow back, then you might have a more sustainable
preserve.

Where'd you study forestry, Phil?

